% A recursion the stack may not hold (docs/plans/plan_bug_analyses.md,
% A1): mini-analyze -bugs, over the call graph of points_to/ (target,
% reach) and the facts of Closure_facts (FACTS.md):
%   list_like(T)       the type T is a variant shaped as a list, not a tree
%   tail(I)            the call I is a tail call as mini-ml compiles it:
%                      its result the function's, outside any try
%   tail_but_try(I)    ... would be one, but for a try around it
%   in_handler(I, T)   the call I is in the handler of the try T
%   in_try(I, T)       ... in its body
% and says, of a call I in F that may come back to F (its target F
% itself, or a function of F's unit that calls back to it):
%   in_try_rec(I, F, T)   I is in a try's body or handler, F's result its:
%                         a loop written, and no tail call by mini-ml,
%                         whose try is a function of its own
%   on_list(I, F)         I is no tail call, F takes a list, and no call
%                         of F back to F is one: a frame an element, as
%                         deep as the input is long
%
%     let rec wait () =                            (021c562b^, Devmnt.ml)
%       try List.assoc tag m.replies
%       with Not_found -> Proc.sleep r; wait ()
%
% In OCaml the call in the handler is a tail call; in mini-ml it is not,
% and each wake of a process waiting on a 9P reply took 48 bytes of a
% kernel stack of 16 KB: rio's windows' processes died after some 330
% replies, in the next process's stack. And
%
%     let rec components = function             (f46346db^, Css_syntax.ml)
%       | [] -> [] | t :: rest -> component t :: components rest
%
% a frame a token, 62,000 on a large style sheet, past the stack.
%
% design:
% Two shapes of the bugs found (docs/plans/bugs/history/), not every
% recursion that is not a tail one: a tree's recursion is as deep as the
% tree, a printer's as its value, and that is fine. A try's is reported
% whatever the type when the call's result is the function's, a loop as
% written, mini-ml making no tail call there (the author's programs
% written for OCaml, where a handler's call is one); an interpreter's
% walk of its tree through a try, its result not returned, is as deep as
% the tree and is not reported. A list's
% when not a tail call, a list being what grows with the input. A string
% walked by an index (String.for_all's go before 2fe7e116, whose && was
% no tail call) is no list: that bug was the compiler's, found by
% checking its tail calls against tail(I) (the plan's A1 check).
% Recursion is within a unit (a let rec and its and's), so the closure
% of the calls is the unit's, small; a recursion through two units is not
% found.
%
% others:
% OCaml's [@tailcall] (4.03) asks the compiler to say when a call it is
% put on is no tail call; Scheme makes every tail call one (R4RS, 1991:
% "properly tail recursive"), which is what mini-ml's try is not. Stack
% depth bounded by analysis is what embedded C's tools do (AbsInt's
% StackAnalyzer, 2000s), and what MISRA's rule against recursion avoids.

% (a library's call written at its caller, model_call, the f of a
% List.map f l, is the library's: in another unit, as it was when
% List.map's own body was read)
own_call(I) :- in_function(I, _), \+ model_call(I, _).
calls(F, G) :- own_call(I), in_function(I, F), target(I, G), alloc_in(F, U), alloc_in(G, U).
back(F, G) :- calls(F, G).
back(F, H) :- back(F, G), calls(G, H).

% the call I in F may come back to F
recursive(I, F) :- own_call(I), in_function(I, F), target(I, F), reach(F).
recursive(I, F) :- own_call(I), in_function(I, F), target(I, G), back(G, F), reach(F).

in_try_rec(I, F, T) :- recursive(I, F), tail_but_try(I), in_try(I, T).
in_try_rec(I, F, T) :- recursive(I, F), tail_but_try(I), in_handler(I, T).
% (let rec walk l f = ...: F a stage of walk, the list an earlier one's)
takes_list(F) :- parameter(F, 1, P), vtype(P, list).
takes_list(F) :- parameter(F, 1, P), vtype(P, T), list_like(T).
takes_list(F) :- inner(F, G), takes_list(G).
% (a function that also comes back by a tail call loops along the list
% and recurses for what is nested, as deep as the nesting: f46346db's fix)
loops(F) :- recursive(I, F), tail(I).
on_list(I, F) :- recursive(I, F), \+ tail(I), takes_list(F), \+ loops(F), \+ in_a_try(I).
in_a_try(I) :- in_try(I, _).
in_a_try(I) :- in_handler(I, _).
